      "writtenOn": "2025-07-06T18:51:50Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Yes, this change to automotive GAS requirements was intentional, and part of increasing automotive OEM adoption of Telephony as part of the Telephony modularization effort. In particular, Automotive devices only require a small subset of Telephony features as opposed to say a phone, which is why the GAS requirements updated some of them (including keepalive) which is consistent with the CDD:\n\n\"SHOULD include support for Cellular keepalive offload.\"\n\nHi Nathan, can you expand a bit on your statement \"It is only required for devices that support the Telephony HAL version...\"? I didn\u0027t see where in the CDD that was mentioned as related to this particular requirement?\n\nThanks!",
      "parentUuid": "36b952ce_afbf27f2",
      "revId": "cd87e53a2dd340ef458a86c0f70846170a1380bd",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "eca87f10_bbaf236f",
        "filename": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
        "patchSetId": 3
      },
      "lineNbr": 0,
      "author": {
        "id": 1060151
      },
      "writtenOn": "2025-07-07T16:33:07Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "The CDD doesn\u0027t say anything about the HAL; however, the CDD is also not all-encompassing. IOW, the absence of a requirement in the CDD doesn\u0027t mean something isn\u0027t required based on a variety of other \"sources\" of requirements. The VSR is one; the API documentation is another; tests themselves are a third. In general, tests and especially API documentation supersede any ambiguity in the CDD.\nUnfortunately I was not privy to the conversations where this requirement was removed from the VSR, so I just can\u0027t be helpful in unraveling the intent there; however, it looks like the signature in the HAL now allows this to be unsupported if the feature flag for DATA is not supported:\n\n    /**\n     * @param info Response info struct containing response type, serial no. and error\n     * @param status Status object containing a new handle and a current status. The status returned\n     *        here may be PENDING to indicate that the radio has not yet processed the keepalive\n     *        request.\n     *\n     * Valid errors returned:\n     *   RadioError:REQUEST_NOT_SUPPORTED when android.hardware.telephony.data is not defined\n     *   RadioError:NONE\n     *   RadioError:NO_RESOURCES\n     *   RadioError:INVALID_ARGUMENTS\n     */\n    void startKeepaliveResponse(in RadioResponseInfo info, in KeepaliveStatus status);\n    \nSo, if the device isn\u0027t declaring telephony.data (which also exempts a huge swath of requirements), then it isn\u0027t required (again, I\u0027m assuming this thing needs GMS core?). As it stands, it looks like this is still required to me (again, assuming a HAL new enough to require telephony.data).\n\nAlso, FWIW, an implementation of this is a couple hundred lines of code (I just saw one), and it has been in the HAL for many years now. As a separate conversation, I\u0027d like to understand why a vendor would still push back on this. It can\u0027t be because of substantial effort, and it doesn\u0027t actually require a hardware change.",
      "parentUuid": "4be45d14_9b971e87",
      "revId": "cd87e53a2dd340ef458a86c0f70846170a1380bd",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "d4859c22_dfa83f06",
        "filename": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
        "patchSetId": 3
      },
      "lineNbr": 0,
      "author": {
        "id": 1470463
      },
      "writtenOn": "2025-07-13T20:54:19Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Hi Nathan,\n\nThanks for the detailed response! I can help with understanding on why we get pushback in Automotive for keepalive. IIUC, many of the benefits of keepalives don\u0027t apply to automotive (power savings are not a concerns, VPNs are not supported, calling/messages are handled by a phone connected over BT, etc).\n\nSince there is little perceived benefit (let me know if there is something I am missing which can help when talking keepalive support with partners), there isn\u0027t similarly little appetite to support keepalives even if the overall implementation surface is small (couple hundred lines of code, etc). \n\nAlso, one of the primary areas of focus for us with our partners is to help with compliance costs, so any help we can give in this area without impacting users is one we are interested in exploring. Thanks and let me know if you have any questions on what I wrote above!",
      "parentUuid": "eca87f10_bbaf236f",
      "revId": "cd87e53a2dd340ef458a86c0f70846170a1380bd",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "84d45881_8e24476d",
        "filename": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
        "patchSetId": 3
      },
      "lineNbr": 0,
      "author": {
        "id": 1060151
      },
      "writtenOn": "2025-07-14T20:18:45Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I think we should turn the discussion around a little bit an ask:\n1) Is the requirement in some way onerous/difficult to meet?\n2) If not, what\u0027s the cost in time/complexity/bugs of fragmenting the ecosystem?\n\nIn this case, the compliance cost is very low, and even if the feature doesn\u0027t get used, its existence does not impose a meaningful resource cost. In fact, we recently had another team demonstrate that even for someone with zero familiarity, it was less than a full day\u0027s work.\n\nMeanwhile, as evidenced by what seems like an not-entirely-successful attempt to exempt vendors from the requirement, our compliance requirements and infrastructure are very complicated (the vendor freeze in particular has piled onto this, but form-factor-specific requirements have also added a substantial amount of pain). Moreover, exempting devices from the requirement is not cost-free even to do it: in order to avoid undefined behavior, exempted devices need to have defined behavior, which also needs to be tested and for which there are not currently tests AFAIK.\n\nWRT benefit, I imagine this will only be used if the board ends up doing some form of WiFi calling/SIP calling, but since we build platforms, it\u0027s really hard to say, and when/if some auto vendors end up using it, another mess will ensue. As such, exempting from requirements really ought to be a last option, not a first option. This appears to be case in point, as it isn\u0027t even this simplest option, and is not devoid of collateral damage. I will provide you an internal link to a reference implementation that perhaps you can adapt and include along with the rest of the min radio. That should reduce vendor compliance cost from very minimal down to zero.",
      "parentUuid": "d4859c22_dfa83f06",
      "revId": "cd87e53a2dd340ef458a86c0f70846170a1380bd",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
